     Searching of the password file is done using the _n_d_b_m  data-
     base access routines.  _S_e_t_p_w_e_n_t opens the database; _e_n_d_p_w_e_n_t
     closes it.  _G_e_t_p_w_u_i_d and _g_e_t_p_w_n_a_m search the database (open-
     ing  it  if  necessary)  for a matching _u_i_d or _n_a_m_e.  EOF is
     returned if there is no entry.

     For programs wishing to read the entire  database,  _g_e_t_p_w_e_n_t
     reads the next line (opening the database if necessary).  In
     addition to opening the database, _s_e_t_p_w_e_n_t can  be  used  to
     make  _g_e_t_p_w_e_n_t  begin  its  search from the beginning of the
     database.

     _S_e_t_p_w_f_i_l_e changes the default password  file  to  _n_a_m_e  thus
     allowing  alternate password files to be used.  Note that it
     does _n_o_t close the  previous  file.   If  this  is  desired,
     _e_n_d_p_w_e_n_t should be called prior to it.

FILES
     /etc/passwd

SEE ALSO
     getlogin(3), getgrent(3), passwd(5)

DIAGNOSTICS
     The routines _g_e_t_p_w_e_n_t, _g_e_t_p_w_u_i_d, and _g_e_t_p_w_n_a_m, return a null
     pointer (0) on EOF or error.

BUGS
     All information is contained in a static area so it must  be
     copied if it is to be saved.

























